From 71c16a90bde6528469838360c35333da75e29e09 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 13:56:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Auto-derive a suffix-stripped alias so a run doesn't depend
 on the user supplying one

A real run for Sonata Software Limited over a 7-month window came back with
zero relevant items despite genuine, easy-to-find coverage (Q4 results, a
CEO change, a 10% single-day price jump) existing in that window. The cause:
the only aliases in play were the company name exactly as typed - "Sonata
Software Limited" - and an unrelated user-supplied guess. Both the relevance
scorer and the slug pre-filter match on literal alias text, and Indian
financial headlines essentially never write out the full legal suffix; they
say "Sonata Software," not "Sonata Software Limited." Verified directly:
scoring a paragraph saturated with "Sonata Software" mentions against the
alias "Sonata Software Limited" alone returns 0.0, "no alias match"; adding
the suffix-stripped "Sonata Software" scores it 0.86.

RunConfig.all_aliases now always tries a version of the company name with a
trailing corporate suffix removed (Limited, Ltd, Pvt Ltd, Private Limited,
Inc, Corp, Corporation, Co, Company, LLC, PLC), so a run does not silently
depend on the user remembering to supply a short form by hand - every
existing README example command already does this manually
(--alias Adani --alias "Adani Group" --alias AEL), which is exactly what
happened not to occur here.

Adds ceia/models.py:_strip_corporate_suffix with direct unit tests, tests
for the all_aliases integration (including that names with no suffix are
unaffected), and an end-to-end regression test reproducing the exact Sonata
Software case through the real relevance scorer. 378 tests passing, up from
366.
